 "steps": [
  {
   "caption": "Remove the arrow: p(x) → W is ¬p(x) ∨ W. The hypothesis is now visibly negated.",
   "patches": [
    {
     "id": "r1",
     "set": {
      "hidden": false
     }
    },
    {
     "id": "y1",
     "set": {
      "hidden": false
     }
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "caption": "W has no free x, so ∀x passes it in a disjunction: ∀x(¬p(x) ∨ W) ≡ (∀x ¬p(x)) ∨ W.",
   "patches": [
    {
     "id": "r2",
     "set": {
      "hidden": false
     }
    },
    {
     "id": "y2",
     "set": {
      "hidden": false
     }
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "caption": "De Morgan for quantifiers: ∀x ¬p(x) is ¬∃x p(x). This is where the ∀ turns into an ∃.",
   "patches": [
    {
     "id": "r3",
     "set": {
      "hidden": false,
      "tone": "accent"
     }
    },
    {
     "id": "y3",
     "set": {
      "hidden": false
     }
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "caption": "Put the arrow back: ¬∃x p(x) ∨ W is ∃x p(x) → W. One p is enough to force W.",
   "patches": [
    {
     "id": "r4",
     "set": {
      "hidden": false,
      "tone": "success",
      "fill": "success"
     }
    },
    {
     "id": "y4",
     "set": {
      "hidden": false
     }
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "caption": "The tempting answer keeps ∀: ∀x p(x) → W (GATE 2020, option C). Test it in the world D = {0, 1}, p = {0}, W = F.",
   "patches": [
    {
     "id": "bad",
     "set": {
      "hidden": false,
      "tone": "danger"
     }
    },
    {
     "id": "world",
     "set": {
